/E (This report was produced from a dataset of 2,012 posts collected from a single platform over twelve weeks. That is a narrow window onto a war that has displaced hundreds of thousands of people, and we want to be clear at the outset about what it can and cannot support.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (It can support a behavioural finding. The network is assessed as aligned with the Rwandan government and M23 messaging at high confidence; as directly tasked by a state organ at low-to-medium. That gap is the boundary of what public data can establish, not a hedge, and readers who collapse it will be reading something we did not publish.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (The report documents three operations, including the Congolese government's own campaign. This is not a verdict on the war. The material at issue is largely not false; the argument is about what was done with facts once their context was stripped. And the named handles are assessed as inauthentic assets, not as identified people.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (The report's sharpest claim is that the standard instrument for measuring influence operations, diffusion, reach, and breakout, is the wrong instrument here, and that an operation reaching almost nobody can still be doing its job. That claim is contestable. We publish it because the alternative is a monitoring environment in which this class of operation is scored as a failure and filed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
