238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Between January and March 2025, the M23 armed movement captured Goma and Bukavu, the two largest cities in eastern Democratic Republic of Congo. The fall of those cities triggered the most intense diplomatic period of the conflict, drawing in the United Nations Security Council, the European Union, the United States, and Qatari mediators. Alongside the fighting on the ground, a parallel battle was waged over how these events would be interpreted by the institutions with the authority to sanction, condemn, or legitimize.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The report's central finding concerns the direction of the covert network's fire. Its purpose was not to persuade the general public but to place a counter-narrative directly in front of two institutional accounts, @UN and @EUCouncil, in the very weeks those institutions were deciding on sanctions. That this is a coordinated, inauthentic network rather than organic activism is established by strong behavioural evidence: machine-speed burst posting, stray script line numbers in published posts, synchronised multi-account activation, and wave timing correlated with EU and UN decision windows. But coordination is the means, not the point. What distinguishes the operation is that its narratives, closely aligned with the strategic messaging of the Rwandan government and M23, were addressed to decision-makers at the moment of decision rather than to the Congolese or Rwandan publics. By conventional engagement metrics the operation appears to have failed, generating 2.1 percent of the dataset's total views. This report argues that engagement is the wrong instrument for this class of operation, and that decision-point targeting of international institutions is a distinct category of impact that existing influence operation frameworks do not adequately capture.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The case is situated within the broader context of documented pro-Rwanda influence operations, the structural limitations of the Great Lakes FIMI monitoring environment, and the constraints of fact-checking as a counter-narrative instrument. In particular, it highlights the challenge posed by manipulation strategies that rely not on falsehoods, but on selectively framed truths disseminated through constructed personas.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
