/T (Each side of the war ran an information campaign alongside its military and diplomatic effort. The Congolese government launched CongolaisTelema \(Lingala for "Congolese, stand up"\), a patriotic mobilisation campaign fronted by Communications Minister Patrick Muyaya, carried on national broadcaster RTNC, and structured around army recruitment, a cultural front of musicians and slam poets, and provincial tours by Prime Minister Judith Suminwa. Rwanda and M23 pressed two core narratives in every diplomatic venue: that the Congolese army is integrated with the FDLR, and that the DRC government persecutes its Tutsi and Banyamulenge citizens. Both narratives have a documented factual substrate. This report examines what occurred when these narratives were operationalised at scale on X, by whom, through what mechanisms, and directed at which audiences.)
/E (Each side of the war ran an information campaign alongside its military and diplomatic effort. The Congolese government launched CongolaisTelema \(Lingala for "Congolese, stand up"\), a patriotic mobilisation campaign fronted by Communications Minister Patrick Muyaya, carried on national broadcaster RTNC, and structured around army recruitment, a cultural front of musicians and slam poets, and provincial tours by Prime Minister Judith Suminwa. Rwanda and M23 pressed two core narratives in every diplomatic venue: that the Congolese army is integrated with the FDLR, and that the DRC government persecutes its Tutsi and Banyamulenge citizens. Both narratives have a documented factual substrate. This report examines what occurred when these narratives were operationalised at scale on X, by whom, through what mechanisms, and directed at which audiences.)
/Pg 18 0 R
