/T (Synchronised account activation reinforces the picture. On the morning of February 28, accounts NKOTANYI1_, TuyisengeD22748, MCishakuri26676, F_Hagenimana, BizimanaGratien and Saidi74224560 all began posting within a two-hour window \(08:24 to 10:40 UTC\), each producing 10 to 28 posts on the same hashtag pair \(#TshisekediAgainstPeace #UNSecurityCouncil\). A similar pattern occurred on March 15, when 25 accounts were active within a single day using #TshisekediIsKilling. Taken together, these behavioural patterns indicate an operation optimised not for audience engagement but for visibility at specific institutional targets. The combination of burst posting, synchronised activation, and centrally distributed messaging suggests a model designed to saturate hashtags at precise moments, particularly those aligned with diplomatic decision windows. This differs from conventional influence operations that seek to build reach or persuasion among broad publics. Instead, the observed activity is consistent with a form of decision-point targeting, in which volume and timing are used to insert specific narratives into the information streams monitored by international organisations. In this model, low engagement is not a failure condition but an expected outcome of a campaign oriented toward elite rather than mass audiences.)
/E (Synchronised account activation reinforces the picture. On the morning of February 28, accounts NKOTANYI1_, TuyisengeD22748, MCishakuri26676, F_Hagenimana, BizimanaGratien and Saidi74224560 all began posting within a two-hour window \(08:24 to 10:40 UTC\), each producing 10 to 28 posts on the same hashtag pair \(#TshisekediAgainstPeace #UNSecurityCouncil\). A similar pattern occurred on March 15, when 25 accounts were active within a single day using #TshisekediIsKilling. Taken together, these behavioural patterns indicate an operation optimised not for audience engagement but for visibility at specific institutional targets. The combination of burst posting, synchronised activation, and centrally distributed messaging suggests a model designed to saturate hashtags at precise moments, particularly those aligned with diplomatic decision windows. This differs from conventional influence operations that seek to build reach or persuasion among broad publics. Instead, the observed activity is consistent with a form of decision-point targeting, in which volume and timing are used to insert specific narratives into the information streams monitored by international organisations. In this model, low engagement is not a failure condition but an expected outcome of a campaign oriented toward elite rather than mass audiences.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
