/Title (The case documented here differs in ways that are both analytically significant and, we argue, increasingly typical. It concerns information manipulation deployed within an active interstate conflict, in a region where platform trust and safety investment is limited, fact-checking capacity is overwhelmed, and the primary audience is not a voting public but the small set of institutions whose decisions determine the cost of military aggression. This is FIMI as a component of war-fighting, and the Great Lakes region is one of its least monitored theatres.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1206 0 R
/Prev 1212 0 R
/Next 1221 0 R
>>
endobj
1221 0 obj
<<
/Title (This report does not document the first pro-Rwanda influence operation on record. Rather, it adds a behaviourally evidenced case to a growing body of documented activity. The prior work was the June 2024 report by the Clemson University Media Forensics Hub, which identified an AI-enabled, RPF-aligned coordinated network of at least 464 accounts on X that produced more than 650,000 posts from January 2024 onward, with the DRC conflict as its dominant content theme. That study established the scale and persistence of pro-Rwanda networked activity within the platform environment.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1206 0 R
/Prev 1220 0 R
/Next 1213 0 R
>>
endobj
1222 0 obj
<<
/Title (The tensions between Rwanda and the Democratic Republic of Congo are not new. Their contemporary form is rooted in the events of 1994. Over approximately one hundred days, an estimated 800,000 people, the majority of them Tutsi, were killed in Rwanda in one of the fastest acts of mass murder in recorded history. The Hutu militias responsible fled across the border into what was then Zaire, settling in the east, keeping their weapons and command structures intact. The armed groups that later consolidated in those eastern forests became the FDLR, a movement rooted in the same ideology that drove the genocide, recruiting across generations and financing itself through the region's mineral trade.3)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1214 0 R
/Next 1229 0 R
>>
endobj
1223 0 obj
<<
/Title (Each side of the war ran an information campaign alongside its military and diplomatic effort. The Congolese government launched CongolaisTelema \(Lingala for "Congolese, stand up"\), a patriotic mobilisation campaign fronted by Communications Minister Patrick Muyaya, carried on national broadcaster RTNC, and structured around army recruitment, a cultural front of musicians and slam poets, and provincial tours by Prime Minister Judith Suminwa. Rwanda and M23 pressed two core narratives in every diplomatic venue: that the Congolese army is integrated with the FDLR, and that the DRC government persecutes its Tutsi and Banyamulenge citizens. Both narratives have a documented factual substrate. This report examines what occurred when these narratives were operationalised at scale on X, by whom, through what mechanisms, and directed at which audiences.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1214 0 R
/Prev 1230 0 R
>>
endobj
