/Title (MATERIALS AND METHODS Data Collection The dataset was collected from X \(formerly Twitter\) under twelve predefined hashtags and keyword collection tags relating to the eastern DRC conflict. These tags were selected to capture three overlapping layers of the information environment: \(1\) general conflict discourse \(e.g., rdc, goma, m23, rwanda, fdlr, rdf\), \(2\) campaign-specific hashtags associated with anti-Tshisekedi narratives, and \(3\) tags linked to identifiable mobilisation efforts such as CongolaisTelema. This approach allows the dataset to capture both organic discussion and coordinated campaign activity within the same analytical frame. After the removal of 110 duplicate rows, the final analysed dataset contains 2,012 unique posts from 794 unique account handles published between January 2 and March 30, 2025, with 3.46 million combined views. Each record includes full text, post URL, timestamp, engagement metrics \(views, likes, retweets, replies, quotes, bookmarks\), language, and author metadata \(display name, bio, follower and following counts, declared location, verification status\).)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1246 0 R
/Next 1253 0 R
>>
endobj
1250 0 obj
<<
/Title (This case complicates a simple model of coordinated inauthentic behaviour as necessarily networked or state-directed. It shows that a single, persistent actor using basic distribution tactics can achieve comparable or greater reach than a structured operation, provided their content is embedded in high-visibility discovery pathways. In the context of conflict-era FIMI, effectiveness is not determined solely by scale or coordination, but by the ability to insert narratives into the informational routes that real audiences already use.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1254 0 R
/Next 1247 0 R
>>
endobj
1251 0 obj
<<
/Title (The rdc tag captures the Congolese government's own information campaign in the final week of March \(with collection for this tag concentrated from March 22 to 30\). CongolaisTelema is overt state communication: it is fronted by named officials, broadcast on state television, and tied to offline events including FARDC recruitment drives, provincial tours by Prime Minister Judith Suminwa, and concerts and slam poetry sessions of the "Front Culturel".)
/Dest [33 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1247 0 R
/Next 1255 0 R
>>
