1287 0 obj
<<
/Title (This pattern is not incidental underperformance but a structural feature of the operation. This collapse is explained by three features. First, the asset base is disposable: accounts with minimal follower counts lack any built-in amplification capacity. Second, there is no evidence of a secondary amplification layer; retweet activity is negligible \(227 retweets across 612 posts\). Third, the network does not attempt to present itself as locally embedded, with many accounts openly signalling Rwandan affiliation and posting primarily in English.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1276 0 R
/Prev 1282 0 R
/Next 1283 0 R
>>
endobj
1288 0 obj
<<
/Title (A master list running to at least 45 numbered entries implies a content volume substantially larger than what this collection captured for that wave. It implies that additional accounts posting from the same script likely exist outside the collection.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1285 0 R
/Next 1289 0 R
>>
endobj
1289 0 obj
<<
/Title (Synchronised account activation reinforces the picture. On the morning of February 28, accounts NKOTANYI1_, TuyisengeD22748, MCishakuri26676, F_Hagenimana, BizimanaGratien and Saidi74224560 all began posting within a two-hour window \(08:24 to 10:40 UTC\), each producing 10 to 28 posts on the same hashtag pair \(#TshisekediAgainstPeace #UNSecurityCouncil\). A similar pattern occurred on March 15, when 25 accounts were active within a single day using #TshisekediIsKilling. Taken together, these behavioural patterns indicate an operation optimised not for audience engagement but for visibility at specific institutional targets. The combination of burst posting, synchronised activation, and centrally distributed messaging suggests a model designed to saturate hashtags at precise moments, particularly those aligned with diplomatic decision windows. This differs from conventional influence operations that seek to build reach or persuasion among broad publics. Instead, the observed activity is consistent with a form of decision-point targeting, in which volume and timing are used to insert specific narratives into the information streams monitored by international organisations. In this model, low engagement is not a failure condition but an expected outcome of a campaign oriented toward elite rather than mass audiences.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1285 0 R
/Prev 1288 0 R
>>
endobj
